  abstract = {This trial compared the efficacy of antiplatelet therapy with prasugrel or clopidogrel in patients with non–ST-segment elevation MI or unstable angina. Although prasugrel provides more intense platelet inhibition, clinical outcomes were similar with the two drugs. Clinical-practice guidelines for patients with acute coronary syndromes consisting of unstable angina or myocardial infarction without ST-segment elevation recommend a strategy of early invasive management (angiography within 48 to 72 hours with provisional revascularization) for patients at moderate to high risk.1,2 However, analyses from clinical trials and national registries have shown that many such patients are treated medically without revascularization and that such patients have poorer long-term cardiovascular outcomes than those who undergo revascularization.3–6 Even though patients with acute coronary syndromes who receive only medical therapy have an increased-risk profile, they have been underrepresented in large-scale, contemporary, randomized .~.~.},
